sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch12-40> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "The axiom systems widely used in mathematics, though usually incomplete, are typically close to complete, because they were built to describe definite kinds of objects; as a result, the alternative objects (nonstandard models) that also satisfy them, such as those for standard arithmetic, differ only in obscure ways.";
    dc:type "mathematical";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Axiom systems used in mathematics are usually close to complete";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Stephen Wolfram, A New Kind of Science (Champaign, IL: Wolfram Media, 2002), Chapter 12, “The Principle of Computational Equivalence”; section “Implications for Mathematics and Its Foundations” (from p. 772), p. 797. Scope: standard axiom systems of mathematics such as arithmetic, group theory and set theory"
}
